The JS reference carries every integer literal as a JS number, so a literal past
2^53 is rounded on its way through the lexer and there is nothing downstream
that can recover it. It renders this fixture as:

  basis=-3750763034362896384     rounded, and printed signed
  past53=9007199254740992        rounded down by one
  max=384                        18446744073709551615 lost outright
  half=192
  wrapped=-5809476064875094016
  t=-5809348521526221540
  u=-5809347422014593329

Reproducing that to match would not be parity worth having. It is also not a
harmless divergence: the basis in question is the FNV-1a seed in
std/core/strings.yoop, so under the reference every string in every program
hashes to the wrong value, and a Map iterates its entries in an order that
depends on it. That is the whole of the map-order difference the program probe
reports on examples/pass/derive_display_array_vec.yoop.

The bootstrap's values agree with the arithmetic done by hand. See
plans/retire_js_compiler.md section B.
